de carries an incumbency advantage, and no Nebraska-specific general-election polls or expert ratings are available for this contest. Headline coverage has centered on the state's Senate and 2nd District races, including Democratic interest in the district-level electoral vote, rather than the statewide presidential outcome.\n\nWith no polling to weigh, the forecast rests on fundamentals: the state's durable Republican lean and an even national environment. That points to an expected Republican margin of roughly ten points, with wide uncertainty given the absence of local survey data. A strong Democratic national wave or unexpected candidate dynamics could narrow the gap, but the structural lean makes Nebraska a likely Republican state in this election.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1258, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1632, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1797, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1802, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1712, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1741, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1653, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1776, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1721, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1732, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1724, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.1714, "margin": -10.0}]}